  "standards": [
    {
      "id": "b7309607-a6e8-4cad-95c0-b9e70499c092",
      "body": "CA_CCSS",
      "code": "F-IF.4",
      "gradeLevel": "HS",
      "domain": "Interpreting Functions",
      "fullText": "4. For a function that models a relationship between two  quantities, interpret key features of graphs and tables in  terms of the quantities, and sketch graphs showing key  features given a verbal description of the relationship.   Key features include: intercepts; intervals where the  function\u00a0is increasing, decreasing, positive, or negative;  relative maximums and minimums; symmetries; end  behavior; and periodicity. \u2605"
    },
    {
      "id": "3b97750b-4fac-47a4-84da-96b01a86274c",
      "body": "CA_CCSS",
      "code": "F-IF.5",
      "gradeLevel": "HS",
      "domain": "Interpreting Functions",
      "fullText": "5. Relate the domain of a function to its graph and, where  applicable, to the quantitative relationship it describes. For  example, if the function h gives the number of person-hours  it takes to assemble n engines in a factory, then the positive  integers would be an appropriate domain for the\u00a0function. \u2605 California High School Function Families  for 200, Alex LESSON 5 Recognizing Functions  by Characteristics \u00a9 Carnegie Learning, Inc. 31 42 53 4 Lesson 5 Topic 1 1 6 42 53 4 Lesson 5 Topic 1 1 6"
    }
  ],
